1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,354
Aarav turns up the bass
knob on his music speaker.

2
00:00:03,654 --> 00:00:05,351
The deep drums get louder.

3
00:00:05,351 --> 00:00:07,333
The high cymbals stay the same.

4
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:10,321
How can a circuit pick
out only the low notes?

5
00:00:10,821 --> 00:00:12,049
Think of a sieve.

6
00:00:12,049 --> 00:00:14,167
Pour in sand and pebbles together.

7
00:00:14,517 --> 00:00:16,418
The fine sand slips through.

8
00:00:16,418 --> 00:00:18,066
The pebbles stay behind.

9
00:00:18,416 --> 00:00:20,298
A filter does this with signals.

10
00:00:20,298 --> 00:00:22,784
It passes some frequencies
and blocks others.

11
00:00:23,434 --> 00:00:27,015
Frequency means how many times
a wave shakes each second.

12
00:00:27,365 --> 00:00:28,971
Low notes shake slowly.

13
00:00:28,971 --> 00:00:30,527
High notes shake fast.

14
00:00:31,177 --> 00:00:33,435
The secret part is the capacitor.

15
00:00:33,785 --> 00:00:37,591
Its opposition to AC is
called reactance, XC.

16
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:40,529
XC equals one over two pi f C.

17
00:00:41,229 --> 00:00:43,558
At low frequency, XC is huge.

18
00:00:43,558 --> 00:00:45,774
The capacitor acts like a wall.

19
00:00:46,124 --> 00:00:48,424
At high frequency, XC is tiny.

20
00:00:48,424 --> 00:00:50,159
It acts like a shortcut.

21
00:00:50,809 --> 00:00:53,878
Let's build a low pass
filter on the breadboard.

22
00:00:54,228 --> 00:00:59,193
A signal generator, a resistor in series,
and a capacitor across the output.

23
00:00:59,543 --> 00:01:03,611
The scope shows the input in
blue, and the output in amber.

24
00:01:03,611 --> 00:01:04,806
In slow motion.

25
00:01:05,156 --> 00:01:08,763
At one hundred hertz, the
output is almost the full input.

26
00:01:09,113 --> 00:01:10,841
Turn up to one kilohertz.

27
00:01:10,841 --> 00:01:13,750
The output drops to about
seventy one percent.

28
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:17,232
At ten kilohertz, only about
ten percent gets through.

29
00:01:17,582 --> 00:01:18,573
Low notes pass.

30
00:01:18,573 --> 00:01:21,076
High notes are shorted
away by the capacitor.

31
00:01:21,726 --> 00:01:24,333
Now swap the resistor and the capacitor.

32
00:01:24,333 --> 00:01:26,687
Take the output across the resistor.

33
00:01:27,037 --> 00:01:28,687
This is a high pass filter.

34
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:30,539
Watch the same three frequencies.

35
00:01:30,889 --> 00:01:34,064
Now low notes are blocked,
and high notes pass.

36
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:35,614
Why?

37
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:37,325
Each filter is a voltage divider.

38
00:01:37,675 --> 00:01:41,791
For the low pass, V out
over V in equals XC over Z.

39
00:01:42,491 --> 00:01:46,533
Z is the impedance, root of
R squared plus XC squared.

40
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:51,008
There is one special
frequency, where XC equals R.

41
00:01:51,358 --> 00:01:54,515
There, V out is one over
root two times V in.

42
00:01:54,515 --> 00:01:56,694
About seventy point seven percent.

43
00:01:57,394 --> 00:01:59,766
This is the cut off frequency, f c.

44
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:01,559
Half the power gets through.

45
00:02:02,259 --> 00:02:05,112
Set one over two pi f C equal to R.

46
00:02:05,812 --> 00:02:08,905
You get f c equals one over two pi RC.

47
00:02:09,605 --> 00:02:12,217
Now plot the output against frequency.

48
00:02:12,217 --> 00:02:14,403
This is the frequency response.

49
00:02:14,753 --> 00:02:18,602
The low pass curve stays
flat, then falls after f c.

50
00:02:18,952 --> 00:02:22,789
The high pass curve rises,
then stays flat after f c.

51
00:02:23,139 --> 00:02:27,846
Both cross at seventy point seven
percent, right at the cut off frequency.

52
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:30,642
Low pass keeps the slow waves.

53
00:02:30,642 --> 00:02:32,787
High pass keeps the fast ones.

54
00:02:33,587 --> 00:02:34,714
Worked example.

55
00:02:34,714 --> 00:02:37,663
Our breadboard used one
point six kilo ohms.

56
00:02:38,013 --> 00:02:41,158
And a capacitor of zero
point one microfarad.

57
00:02:41,508 --> 00:02:44,071
f c equals one over two pi RC.

58
00:02:44,771 --> 00:02:48,704
Two pi times sixteen hundred
times zero point one millionth.

59
00:02:49,054 --> 00:02:53,446
That is about one point zero zero
five times ten to the minus three.

60
00:02:54,096 --> 00:02:57,759
One over that is about nine
hundred ninety five hertz.

61
00:02:57,759 --> 00:02:59,286
Nearly one kilohertz.

62
00:02:59,936 --> 00:03:04,147
That is why the output fell to
seventy one percent at one kilohertz.

63
00:03:04,797 --> 00:03:06,261
Now, the exam trap.

64
00:03:06,611 --> 00:03:10,297
Many students say that at cut
off, the output is half the input.

65
00:03:10,647 --> 00:03:11,547
It is not.

66
00:03:11,513 --> 00:03:14,521
The output voltage is
seventy point seven percent.

67
00:03:14,871 --> 00:03:17,044
It is the power that falls to half.

68
00:03:17,694 --> 00:03:19,072
Where is this asked?

69
00:03:19,072 --> 00:03:20,450
In PUC board papers.

70
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:25,345
Draw the low pass or high pass
circuit, its response curve, and find f

71
00:03:25,345 --> 00:03:26,245
c.

72
00:03:26,401 --> 00:03:27,301
Try this.

73
00:03:27,269 --> 00:03:32,266
A high pass filter has R equal to two
kilo ohms and C equal to zero point

74
00:03:32,266 --> 00:03:33,833
zero five microfarad.

75
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:37,549
A ten volt signal comes in
at the cut off frequency.

76
00:03:37,549 --> 00:03:39,466
Find f c and the output.

77
00:03:39,466 --> 00:03:41,869
Take pi as three point one four.

78
00:03:47,269 --> 00:03:48,169
B.

79
00:03:47,709 --> 00:03:52,618
About one point five nine kilohertz,
and seven point zero seven volts.

80
00:03:52,968 --> 00:03:55,531
f c equals one over two pi RC.

81
00:03:55,881 --> 00:04:00,814
Two pi times two thousand times zero
point zero five millionth is six point

82
00:04:00,814 --> 00:04:03,610
two eight times ten to the minus four.

83
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,531
One over that is about fifteen
hundred and ninety hertz.

84
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:10,860
At cut off, V out is ten over root two.

85
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:13,285
About seven point zero seven volts.

86
00:04:13,935 --> 00:04:15,481
Screenshot this card.

87
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:20,046
Next, energy bands.

88
00:04:20,046 --> 00:04:23,173
Why does copper carry
current, but glass does not?
